<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durga is a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess, a warrior form of the divine mother who is celebrated for slaying the buffalo demon Mahishasura. She is usually shown riding a lion or tiger and holding weapons in many arms. Her worship is especially strong in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the autumn festival of Durga Puja is the principal religious celebration, and many royal courts adopted her as a protective deity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durga.jpg" alt="Durga" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sri Shilpi Siddanthi Siddalinga Swami (1885 - 1952) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahishasura-Mardini_Durga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Durga's name means the impassable or unassailable one and is related to durg, a fortress. Words of this root occur in the Vedic literature, but the Vedic texts lack the legends later told of her. Asko Parpola reads a cylindrical seal from Kalibangan as showing a Durga-like goddess of war associated with the tiger, which would take her image back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a>. In 1916 the historian Ramaprasad Chanda proposed that she developed from a mountain goddess worshipped by the dwellers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> and the Vindhyas. Inscriptions of the 6th century CE already mention her victory over Mahishasura, and the Devi Mahatmya, composed between about 400 and 600 CE according to scholars, presents her as a well-established demon-slaying goddess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the Devi Mahatmya and the Devi Bhagavata Purana, the central texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a>, Durga is the embodiment of creation, preservation and destruction and is identified with Brahman, the ultimate reality. She is best known as Mahishasura-mardini, the slayer of the half-buffalo demon Mahishasura, who had won from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> the boon that only a woman could kill him. The gods gave form to their combined energy as a many-armed warrior woman, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> gave her a lion, and she killed the demon with her trident when he took buffalo form.</p><p>She is shown riding a lion or tiger with between eight and eighteen hands, each holding a weapon given by a male god, yet her face is calm. Shakta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> read the weapons as symbols of self-discipline, prayer and devotion. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> she is a mother, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Kartikeya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a>, although she is generally regarded as independent and not the consort of any god.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Her worship is most prominent in the east and north-east of the subcontinent. Durga Puja, held in the month of Ashvina, usually in September or October, is a major festival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>; clay images are worshipped for nine days and then immersed in water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> of the 14th century give rules for it, and royalty and wealthy families sponsored public celebrations from at least the 16th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> the festival is Dashain.</p><p>Images of her slaying the buffalo demon have been found at Buddhist sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in Indonesia at least 135 statues of Durga, dating from the 6th century onwards, have been found. Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a> drew on her image as mother goddess.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within two centuries their craftsmen moved from the rock-cut caves of Badami, excavated in the late sixth century, to free-standing stone temples at Aihole, such as the Lad Khan and <b>Durga</b> temples, and then to the mature complex at Pattadakal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The major traditions are devoted to Vishnu, who descends to earth in incarnations (avatars) such as Rama and Krishna; to Shiva, the ascetic and the lord of creation and destruction; and to the Goddess, Devi, in forms such as <b>Durga</b>, Kali, Lakshmi and Parvati.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Ventan, identified with Indra, Korravai, identified with <b>Durga</b>, and Varunan, the sea god, also appear.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name comes from an epithet of the goddess <b>Durga</b>, &quot;the slayer of the demon Mahisha&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mahishasura-mardini-stotra">Mahishasura Mardini Stotra</a></li><li><blockquote>Later stanzas name Hindu goddesses, <b>Durga</b> among them, and in 1937 this became a matter of dispute.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings ruled the hills through local chiefs and the plains from Jaintiapur, combining Pnar custom with Hindu court ritual and worship of the goddess <b>Durga</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaintia-kingdom">Jaintia Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
